---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';

const path = '/apps/yotpo-email-sms';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'apps/yotpo-email-sms');
const desc = description(site, 'apps/yotpo-email-sms');

---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Supplier guide · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Yotpo Email & SMS: retired product and migration guide</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Yotpo Email & SMS is a retirement and migration decision, not a new messaging purchase. Keep the decision about Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty separate from the decision about which platform now sends email and texts.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Retirement status and what remains distinct</h2>
      <p>Yotpo’s <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">September 2025 migration announcement</a> scheduled full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. The current <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">decommissioning notice</a> says the SMS dashboard and background services are inactive. Do not buy, activate or plan new flows around the retired product.</p>
      <p>That retirement does not mean you must replace Reviews or Loyalty. Their data can still inform communications sent elsewhere: Yotpo documents <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/integrations/attentive/">Attentive integration</a> for review and loyalty data and <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/integrations/omnisend/">Omnisend integration</a>. Verify plan access, supported fields and sync behavior for each module rather than assuming the old unified dashboard survives.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Choose the replacement around data you actually use</h2>
      <p>Yotpo named Attentive and Omnisend as migration partners in its announcement. That is a vendor recommendation, not an independent performance award or a guarantee that an old migration offer remains available. Ask each supplier to demonstrate your required audiences, suppression behavior and message workload before choosing.</p>
      <p>A beauty store with years of licensed customer photos needs to check asset rights, product matching and review-status history. A loyalty-heavy pet store needs current balances, tier transitions and reward rules. An apparel store that uses neither module can evaluate a messaging replacement without buying or moving reviews merely to recreate suite consolidation.</p>
      <p>Read <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-yotpo-email-sms">Klaviyo vs Yotpo Email & SMS</a> and <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-yotpo-email-sms">Omnisend vs Yotpo Email & SMS</a> as related comparison routes; confirm current claims against retirement status before acting. No old ranking, module price estimate or migration success story is evidence of present availability.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Recover the migration record before rebuilding</h2>
      <p>Inventory the systems you use now, including any replacement already installed. Locate archived exports, suppression lists, permission records, templates, segment definitions, sender records and billing agreements. With the old SMS dashboard inactive, do not assume you can log in and export missing data; ask support what recovery remains possible and mark unavailable records explicitly.</p>
      <p>Separate email and SMS eligibility, preserve withdrawal timestamps, and reconcile identities before importing. A loyalty member or product reviewer is not automatically a marketing subscriber. Importing a list should not reactivate suppressed customers. Assign one system of record for preferences and test how changes propagate to each sender.</p>
      <p>Map old automations to destination events rather than copying template names. Record event timestamps, order identifiers and cancellation conditions. Disable duplicate sends from any remaining integrations before enabling replacements. Retain an audit of which flows were rebuilt, replaced or intentionally retired.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Four proposed checks for Reviews and Loyalty continuity</h2>
      <p><strong>Welcome:</strong> on a supplement-store test profile, check that the loyalty introduction shows the actual earning rule and correct enrollment link. Record marketing permission separately from program membership. Measure first orders and enrollment independently if you later run an experiment.</p>
      <p><strong>VIP cart:</strong> for an illustrative $89 protein cart, confirm that a Gold-tier audience is current, that the points balance is accurate, and that purchase cancels queued reminders. Verify separate SMS eligibility; status in the loyalty program cannot supply it.</p>
      <p><strong>Review request:</strong> trace a delivered order through the review-request owner and the replacement sender. Prevent duplicate requests from Reviews and the ESP. Check review completion, delivery timing and product identity before adding a later cross-sell. Fulfillment alone is not proof of delivery.</p>
      <p><strong>Points expiry:</strong> for a hypothetical member with 600 points expiring in 14 days, validate expiration and redemption rules at send time. Cancel or update stale messages after redemption. Review data and Loyalty data have separate contracts: passing one integration test does not validate the other. These are acceptance exercises, with no observed conversion or redemption results claimed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Cost the surviving stack and verify completion</h2>
      <p>Price retained Reviews and Loyalty contracts separately from the new sender. Include profile or send charges, SMS segments, carrier fees, implementation work, integration monitoring and any overlap in contracts. Compare both normal and peak months at the same audience and send workload. Retired Email & SMS module estimates cannot price the new stack.</p>
      <p><strong>Illustrative migration budget:</strong> $300 one-time setup plus $50 extra monthly operating cost totals $900 over twelve months. At $30 contribution per incremental order, covering that amount requires 30 incremental orders over the year. This is planning arithmetic, not supplier pricing or a prediction of migration gains.</p>
      <p>Complete migration when permission and suppression reconciliation, event mapping, sender setup, purchase cancellation, reply ownership and module-data checks pass. Then compare complaints, delivery failures and contribution with a documented baseline. Use randomized eligible comparisons if estimating causal lift; attributed revenue alone cannot prove that retaining a module or switching senders helped.</p>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="/use-cases/loyalty">loyalty campaigns guide</a> and <a class="link" href="/use-cases/reviews-and-ugc">reviews and UGC guide</a> provide related planning context. Preserve useful reviews and reward history while choosing a current sending platform on its own merits.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
